WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.180
(inquisitive music)

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:05.850
- So, if you've been looking to fly

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:07.890
from America to Europe this year,

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:10.440
you'll have noticed that there's
a whole bunch of airlines

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:13.440
that seem to suddenly be
offering very cheap fares.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.630
Airlines like Norse, French Bee, and Play

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:17.460
have all been trying to capture a wave

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:20.730
of post pandemic revenge
spending transatlantic travelers

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:23.370
by trying to lower their ticket
prices as much as possible.

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:26.070
And their tactics go far, far further

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:27.840
than just charging for the seats

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.670
and separating out added extras.

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:33.270
- It's more about getting
every part of your business

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:34.800
at the lowest possible costs.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.480
- But major legacy airlines

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.240
are competing hard on pricing, so much so

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.570
that sometimes they even
undercut the low-cost carriers.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:45.570
And now with winter around the
corner and demand set to fall

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:48.390
there's a huge question
over the long-term viability

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:50.130
of low-cost transatlantic flight.

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:52.140
So are these airlines and their airfares

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:54.390
set to disappear as
quickly as they arrived?

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:55.223
Let's find out.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.640
Okay, so first I want to know
how these low cost airlines

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.650
are getting their ticket prices down.

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:04.500
And so to do that, I've come
to visit Norse Atlantic.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.930
The company was formed in
Norway after the pandemic

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:10.260
and has 15 of these
Boeing 787 Dreamliners,

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:13.230
10 of which it currently
operates from America to Europe.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:16.290
Now, part of what helps
Norse get its prices down

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:19.020
is by doing something called
unbundling ancillaries,

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:22.350
which in plain English is
basically charging separately

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:24.570
for everything other than the seat.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.070
Flying its most basic fare,

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:28.410
Norse will charge you to check in a bag,

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:29.760
or to bring a carry on,

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:31.920
or to choose which seat
you want to sit in.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:33.330
Meals will cost extra,

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:35.310
as will priority boarding and check-in.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:38.250
Norse makes around $87 per passenger

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:40.380
on ancillaries on every flight,

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:42.900
around a fifth of all passenger revenue.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.480
And has aims to become the
highest ancillary revenue

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.760
per passenger airline globally.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.670
But any airline, including
the big legacy carriers,

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:53.910
can, and often do, unbundle
their ticket prices.

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:55.980
So much so that revenue from ancillaries

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:59.520
across the aviation industry
has doubled since 2013

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:02.040
to total over a hundred billion dollars.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:05.340
So to really get prices down,
especially as a startup,

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.290
you need to go even further.

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:08.123
And for Norse,

00:02:08.123 --> 00:02:10.710
that started with getting
these aircraft for cheap.

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:12.660
- It was really an asset play initially

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:15.840
where we got the assets
at historic low prices

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:17.370
and very flexible terms

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:19.230
- [George] That's CEO Bjorn Tore Larsen

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:21.780
who told me that Norse
has streamlined operations

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:23.760
in an attempt to minimize costs.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:25.890
- We fly long haul only,

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:29.040
we fly one aircraft type,
the Dreamliner, only.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:32.460
So all our crew are trained
on only one type of aircraft,

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.440
we only do one type of maintenance.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.330
- The company also operates
some of its flights

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:37.590
from cheaper airports,

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:39.900
like flying out of Gatwick
instead of Heathrow,

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.240
though they do have a
slot at New York's JFK.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:45.510
And instead of flying out
of and back to a fixed hub,

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:48.450
Norse's planes fly onward
from each destination.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:51.030
- You don't have a lot of
costs that is associated

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:54.540
with a hub and spoke
operation where you are

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:57.180
into a much, much more complex
operation straight away.

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:58.500
- Now, all of these factors

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.660
have helped Norse get pricing down

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:03.690
and achieve profitability
for the first time this year.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:05.130
But here's the thing, (snaps)

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:07.950
Norse isn't actually
always that much cheaper

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:10.290
than major legacy US carriers.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:12.870
See, airline prices vary wildly

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:15.300
depending on how far in advance you book,

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:16.470
how long your stay is,

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:19.470
and whether you plan on
flying one-way or return.

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:22.110
But if you compare the
prices for this flight

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:24.840
between Norse, Delta, and United,

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:29.840
Norse averaged around
$615 and United was 660.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:33.300
Now if you include the fact
that Delta and United's price

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:36.480
includes a carry-on bag and a meal,

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.420
which would cost you
upwards of $50 on Norse,

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:42.030
then that price gap soon starts to shrink.

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:43.830
So how are these legacy carriers

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:45.630
able to get their prices down?

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:48.450
- Because of the scale and the
amount of capacity they have

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:50.490
and that brings their unit cost down.

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:51.630
- [George] That's John Grant,

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:54.240
Chief Analyst at aviation data firm OAG.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:58.710
- If a legacy airline has
got 1,500 economy seats

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:01.110
spread across seven flights during the day

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:03.150
then they're able to match that.

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:05.460
- Another reason is that
economy seats only make up

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:08.460
a small fraction of
legacy airlines cabins,

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:11.070
the rest being far more
profitable premium economy,

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:13.050
business, and first class seats.

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:14.580
And that kind of explains why

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:16.980
when I search for tickets around Labor Day

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:20.310
Norse was more expensive
than some major US rivals.

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:22.710
- Sometimes our prices
are going up a little bit

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:26.070
and that is basically because
the cabin is being filled.

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:30.240
So the last passenger is
actually paying quite a bit more

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:31.380
than the first passenger.

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:33.390
- Now recently, demand has been so strong

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:36.450
that there's been enough flyers
for both low cost carriers

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:38.490
and legacy carriers to be able to profit.

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:40.980
- The relative strength of the US dollar

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:44.220
has encouraged a lot of
North American visitors.

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:47.130
And we've been blessed
with just enough capacity.

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:49.200
- But as the summer
season draws to a close,

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:51.780
international demand is set to drop.

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:53.010
And that's got some wondering

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:55.650
whether these low cost long haul carriers

00:04:55.650 --> 00:04:57.300
will be able to survive the winter.

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:58.713
Is it sustainable long term?

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:00.720
- No.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:03.030
- [George] After all, other
airlines have in the past

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:06.180
tried and failed to make low
cost transatlantic flight

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:08.100
commercially sustainable.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:10.050
For years Norwegian Air used to operate

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:12.420
a budget friendly route
between Europe and America.

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:14.490
But the company faced a
whole bunch of headwinds,

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:16.200
from operating Boeing planes

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:18.330
that were grounded during the MAX crisis

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:20.700
to trying to juggle a wide array of roots.

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:22.800
- They had embarked on rapid growth.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:25.560
They had a short haul network
and a long haul network.

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:27.990
They spread themselves
too thinly too quickly.

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:28.920
- During the pandemic,

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:31.440
the company filed for
bankruptcy protection,

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:36.090
restructured, reduced its
fleet from 156 aircraft to 51,

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:39.270
and crucially, discontinued
long haul operations.

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:42.202
What's not certainly is how
sustainable this is long term.

00:05:42.202 --> 00:05:43.860
What are your views?

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:45.090
- You're absolutely right,

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:49.860
It's yet to be proven and
it has not a great history.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:51.060
- But here's the thing,

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:55.380
many of Norse's planes are
actually old Norwegian planes.

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:58.140
So is the company set
to suffer the same fate?

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.900
- I don't totally agree that
we are born out of the ashes,

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:02.520
because we are a brand new airline.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:04.320
We have no hub and spoke operations,

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:07.080
we don't own, you know,
short haul operations

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:09.450
trying to put everything together.

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:10.560
It's very different.

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:11.970
- You still have winter coming,

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:14.280
and you've said yourself,
you know, demand fluctuates.

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:16.470
Are you worried about
some of the winter months?

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:18.120
- I'm always worried, that's my job,

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:19.200
you know, I'm born worried.

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:21.660
And we really have to be very careful.

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:24.810
It's easy to burn a lot of
money if you get things wrong.

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:27.270
- So how can these low cost
carriers survive winter?

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:29.550
Well, one option is to
chase the warmer weather

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:31.080
and adjust your routes.

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:33.210
And Norse plan on doing just that.

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:35.760
Opening up winter routes
to Barbados, Jamaica,

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:38.130
and even adding a new
route to Bangkok in Asia.

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:41.070
But shifting key routes when
you don't have many planes

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:42.600
can create another headache.

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:45.120
- Dipping in and dipping out of markets

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:49.290
is a relatively high risk strategy

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:52.620
because your brand
awareness and your presence

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:55.440
is not as large as the other carriers

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:56.580
who are there year round.

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:59.760
- So another tactic is to
try and carry more cargo,

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:01.710
providing there's more
cargo to be carried.

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:04.950
- We are definitely seeing
a better cargo market

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:07.860
typically during the
winter, but you don't know.

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:09.330
So you can't take that for granted.

00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:12.510
- And if those fail, well,
hunker down for the cold months

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:14.280
and conduct your scheduled maintenance

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:15.870
while your fleet is out of use.

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:18.540
Norse told me that they'd
try all of these tactics,

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:21.570
but the aviation industry
can be pretty cutthroat.

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:24.540
And while low-cost carriers
brace for the winter,

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:27.480
legacy carriers will
capture every advantage.

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:29.820
- If the market becomes
a little bit tougher

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:31.537
those legacy carriers are not gonna say,

00:07:31.537 --> 00:07:32.550
"The market's yours."

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:34.560
They're gonna compete
for every seat with you,

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:36.279
and that's a challenge.

00:07:36.279 --> 00:07:39.112
(rhythmic music)

